  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify by separating out the factors, and use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=1\n$$",
